<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
=============================================================================
 CardDemo modernization: Maven module descriptor for the REPORTING AND
 STATEMENT bounded context, published as com.carddemo:reporting-service and
 rooted at the Java package com.carddemo.reporting.

 PURPOSE
 One Spring Boot service that re-expresses four COBOL programs. Each is cited
 by path and line so a maintainer can diff intent against the baseline:

   app/cbl/CORPT00C.cbl   Online report request screen. Its ad hoc job
                          submission is assembled as PIC X(80) JCL card image
                          literals at L100 to L125 and handed to CICS with
                          EXEC CICS WRITEQ TD QUEUE('JOBS') at L517 to L518.
   app/cbl/CBTRN03C.cbl   Transaction detail report writer. FD-REPTFILE-REC is
                          PIC X(133) at L85, which is where the 133 column
                          report width comes from; COPY CVTRA07Y. at L113.
   app/cbl/CBSTM03A.CBL   Statement generator, writing TWO formats:
                          FD-STMTFILE-REC PIC X(80) at L45 and
                          FD-HTMLFILE-REC PIC X(100) at L47.
   app/cbl/CBSTM03B.CBL   Statement subprogram, CALLed from CBSTM03A at L351,
                          L377 and L401.

 SUPPORTING CONTRACTS
   app/cpy/CVTRA07Y.cpy   The 133 column report layout and its COBOL edit
                          masks: L30 TRAN-REPORT-AMT PIC -ZZZ,ZZZ,ZZZ.ZZ, and
                          L54, L60, L66 PIC +ZZZ,ZZZ,ZZZ.ZZ.
   app/cpy/COSTM01.CPY    The 350 byte card ordered statement view. TRNX-KEY
                          at L21 to L23 is card number then transaction id,
                          and the trailing FILLER PIC X(20) at L36 occupies
                          bytes 331 to 350.
   app/jcl/CREASTMT.JCL   L87 to L91 STMTFILE with DCB LRECL=80, and L92 to
                          L96 HTMLFILE with DCB LRECL=100: the origin of the
                          two statement output formats.
   app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD   L499 to L505 DEFINE TDQUEUE(JOBS), the submission
                          path this service replaces.

 Every path above is REFERENCE-ONLY: the COBOL baseline is the behavioural
 oracle for functional parity and is never modified by this migration. The
 uppercase .CBL, .CPY and .JCL spellings are the on disk spellings, so a
 lowercase citation is a dead reference.

 WHAT THIS MODULE OWNS
 Nothing in the database: no schema, no table, no index, no constraint and no
 grant. Alone among the nine aggregator modules it owns no relational objects.
 It reads read-only cross-schema views through a database role that holds
 SELECT-only grants, and both the views and the grants are bootstrapped by
 data-migration/sql/V0__schemas_and_roles.sql.

 Assumptions: XML 1.0 §2.5 forbids two consecutive hyphens anywhere inside a
 comment, and this file was verified against that rule with xmllint. The house
 comment style used at .gitignore L40 to L65 separates clauses with a double
 hyphen and draws hyphen rulers, and BOTH of those forms are hard not well-formed
 parse errors in XML. This file therefore deviates from house style
 deliberately: rules are drawn with '=', mid-rules with '~', line ranges are
 written "L499 to L505", and prose takes a colon or the word "because" where it
 would naturally take a dash. Consequence of ignoring this: a future reader who
 "restores" the house dash style makes this POM unparseable, and because Maven
 reads it before doing anything else, that breaks every build in the entire
 nine module aggregator rather than only this module.
=============================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
  Assumptions: relativePath is left at its default of ../pom.xml, which resolves
  the aggregator at services/pom.xml on disk. The grandparent
  org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-parent 4.1.0 is reached through
  that aggregator and supplies Java 21, the UTF-8 source and reporting
  encodings that the Checkstyle Checker charset must agree with, and every
  managed dependency version, so nothing here restates any of them. An empty
  relativePath element was rejected because com.carddemo:carddemo-services is
  never published to a repository, so a repository lookup for it could only
  fail. The consequence of this choice is a hard layout contract: this module
  must remain a direct child directory of services/.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo-services</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>reporting-service</artifactId>
  <name>CardDemo Reporting Service</name>
  <description>
    Transaction reports and account statements for the CardDemo migration.
    Serves the 133 column transaction detail report and the plain text plus
    HTML statement pair, and starts on demand report executions. Reads
    read-only cross-schema views under a SELECT-only database role and owns no
    relational objects of its own.
  </description>

  <dependencies>

    <!--
    Refactoring Rationale: Rule T2 of the migration replaces the COBOL copybook
    include path with Java imports, so one former COPY statement becomes exactly
    one type import from the single package that owns that contract. Under COBOL
    every program that needed a layout re-included it, which meant a layout
    change had to be re-verified program by program; a shared kernel module
    makes those contracts single-sourced instead. Money, codecs, the error
    model, the keyset page envelope, the JWT role converter, the timestamp
    formatter and the validation flags are consumed only from
    com.carddemo.common.* and are never re-declared in this module:
    money.Money and money.MoneyModule, codec.CopybookLayout and
    codec.FixedWidthCodec, error.ApiError and error.GlobalExceptionHandler and
    error.AbendDetail, web.CorrelationIdFilter and web.PageResponse,
    security.JwtRoleConverter, observability.MetricsConfig,
    time.TimestampFormatter, validation.DateEditValidator and
    validation.FieldValidationFlag.

    This is the ONLY intra-project dependency. No module may depend on another
    service module, and that prohibition is an ArchUnit test rather than a
    convention, precisely because it would otherwise be tempting here: every
    view this module reads is derived from tables that other services own.
    Agreement with those services is reached through the physical database
    views, never through compiled code.

    Assumptions: the parent aggregator does not manage the intra-project
    coordinate com.carddemo:common-lib in its dependencyManagement, so the
    version is stated on this one dependency. The value used is the
    project.version property inherited from the parent element above, so the
    module version and the sibling version are the same expression and cannot
    drift apart; a literal version string could.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: JPA is present in a module that never writes, which looks
    contradictory until the mapping is named. Hibernate arrives with this
    starter and supplies the Immutable annotation that marks each read-only
    view projection, so an accidental write is rejected in the persistence
    layer instead of travelling to the database and failing there against the
    SELECT-only grant. The projections are views, not tables, so schema
    generation stays off and this module contributes no DDL.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: the report request carries a date range, held in the baseline
    as the PARM-START-DATE and PARM-END-DATE card image fields at
    app/cbl/CORPT00C.cbl L105 to L112. Those dates were edited by COBOL
    paragraph logic before the job was ever submitted, so the same rejection
    must happen before an execution starts here. Bean Validation supplies the
    declarative half and com.carddemo.common.validation.DateEditValidator
    supplies the calendar half; both are needed because neither expresses the
    other's rules.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: identity is a validated Cognito JWT, and the cognito:groups
    claim is converted to Spring Security authorities by
    com.carddemo.common.security.JwtRoleConverter. This replaces the
    CDEMO-USER-TYPE field of the CICS communication area at
    app/cpy/COCOM01Y.cpy L26, whose admin and user domain is fixed by the
    condition names at L27 and L28 of that copybook. The replacement is a
    security correction rather than a like for like port: the communication
    area at L19 to L44 was storage the terminal echoed back, so a client could
    in principle assert its own user type, whereas a signed group claim cannot
    be asserted by the client at all.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: two independent consumers probe the /actuator/health endpoint
    this starter publishes, and they fail in different places. The container
    HEALTHCHECK in this module's Dockerfile uses it to decide whether the task
    is serving, and the ALB target group uses it to decide whether to route to
    the task at all. Removing this dependency therefore breaks the container
    health check and the load balancer health check together, and the second
    failure looks like a networking fault rather than a missing dependency,
    which is why the coupling is recorded here rather than left to be
    rediscovered.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: this module connects to the same Aurora PostgreSQL cluster as
    the six owning services, but under a role holding SELECT-only grants, and
    it reads only. That is the deliberate asymmetry against those services: a
    driver is required, a migration tool is not. That absence is not an
    oversight and is argued in the consolidated absences block below.
    Runtime scope is used because no compiled class in this module names a
    driver type; only the connection URL, supplied at runtime, selects it.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: the contract this publishes is OpenAPI 3.1, authored at
    src/main/resources/openapi/reporting-api.yaml, and ui/src/api/reporting.ts
    is written against it. That makes it a cross-boundary contract consumed by
    a separately built and separately deployed artifact, not internal
    documentation, so a breaking edit to it breaks the browser client rather
    than merely dating a document.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
      <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: metrics carry the service, environment and version common tags
    applied centrally by com.carddemo.common.observability.MetricsConfig, so
    this module adds a registry and no tagging logic of its own. The baseline
    had no metrics tier at all, only job log SYSOUT, so nothing here is a port
    and there is no legacy naming to preserve. Runtime scope is used because
    the registry is discovered by autoconfiguration and no compiled class in
    this module names a Prometheus type.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Refactoring Rationale: the baseline submitted an ad hoc report job by
    writing 80 byte JCL card images to an extrapartition transient data queue.
    app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD defines that queue at L499 to L505, with
    DDNAME(INREADER) and, critically, ERROROPTION(IGNORE) both on L501 and
    RECORDSIZE(80) on L502. Three things were wrong with that approach, and all
    three are why the transport changes instead of being reimplemented.

    First, ERROROPTION(IGNORE) silently swallows write failures, so a request
    could be accepted at the screen and never reach the job entry subsystem
    with nothing anywhere recording the loss. app/cbl/CORPT00C.cbl even carries
    an 'Unable to Write TDQ (JOBS)...' message at L531 that the IGNORE option
    makes unreachable for exactly the failure it names. Second, the request was
    serialised as raw JCL text rather than as data: the card images are built
    as PIC X(80) VALUE literals in double quotes at L100 to L125, so the sort
    field positions TRAN-CARD-NUM,263,16,ZD on L100 and TRAN-PROC-DT,305,10,CH
    on L102 were compiled into presentation strings. Third, the queue is fire
    and forget, leaving no handle with which to observe, address or retry a run.

    states:StartExecution answers all three: it accepts typed input instead of
    card images, it returns an execution ARN that makes the run observable and
    addressable, and it raises an exception instead of ignoring an error, so a
    failed submission can no longer be lost.
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
      <artifactId>sfn</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: this module writes three fixed width artifacts to object
    storage, and every one of those widths is a contract inherited from the
    baseline rather than a choice made here. app/jcl/CREASTMT.JCL declares
    STMTFILE with DCB LRECL=80 at L87 to L91 and HTMLFILE with DCB LRECL=100 at
    L92 to L96, which is the origin of statements being produced in BOTH a
    plain text and an HTML form; app/cbl/CBSTM03A.CBL agrees exactly, carrying
    FD-STMTFILE-REC PIC X(80) at L45 and FD-HTMLFILE-REC PIC X(100) at L47. The
    transaction detail report is the third artifact, at the 133 columns fixed by
    FD-REPTFILE-REC PIC X(133) at app/cbl/CBTRN03C.cbl L85. Object storage
    replaces the catalogued sequential datasets those DD statements allocated,
    so the bytes are preserved even though the container for them is not.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
      <artifactId>s3</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: every endpoint and identifier this service needs is resolved at
    runtime from Terraform outputs, because no service hard-codes an endpoint.
    Parameter Store supplies the non secret values, namely the datasource URL,
    the state machine ARN for the report execution, the output bucket name and
    the JWT issuer URI; Secrets Manager supplies the database credentials for
    the SELECT-only role and the customer managed key references. That split is
    what makes the project constraint of no committed secrets structurally true
    rather than merely observed: there is no configuration file in this module
    into which a credential could be pasted, so none of these values appears in
    application.yml, in a tfvars file or anywhere else under version control.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-parameter-store</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-secrets-manager</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: a controller slice test has no token exchange available to it,
    yet authorization is part of the behaviour under test, since the report and
    statement endpoints are guarded by the cognito:groups authorities described
    at the security starter above. This artifact mints a JWT directly, so the
    slice exercises the same JwtRoleConverter path production uses rather than
    disabling security to reach the handler, which would leave the guard
    untested precisely where it matters.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-security-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Trade-offs: a real PostgreSQL container is materially slower to start than
    an in-memory database, and that cost is accepted because an in-memory
    database cannot prove either of the two properties on which this module's
    correctness actually rests.

    It cannot prove that the SELECT-only database role genuinely CANNOT write,
    because a substitute engine does not implement the same grant model and
    would report success on precisely the write that production must reject,
    turning the one test that guards this module's read-only contract into a
    test that passes for the wrong reason. And it cannot prove that the
    read-only views return the required card number then processing date
    ordering, which is the baseline's own key order rather than a preference:
    TRNX-KEY at app/cpy/COSTM01.CPY L21 to L23 is card number then transaction
    id, and the sort fields compiled into app/cbl/CORPT00C.cbl name
    TRAN-CARD-NUM at position 263 on L100 and TRAN-PROC-DT at position 305 on
    L102. A slower test that is able to fail for the right reason is worth more
    than a fast one that cannot fail at all.

    Assumptions: these are the Testcontainers 2.x coordinates. The 2.x line
    renamed every module to the testcontainers prefixed form, so the 1.x names
    'postgresql' and 'junit-jupiter' are absent from
    org.testcontainers:testcontainers-bom 2.0.5, which the parent imports.
    Verified directly against that BOM rather than assumed: it declares
    testcontainers-junit-jupiter and testcontainers-postgresql and does not
    declare the bare names at all. Using the 1.x names would leave both
    dependencies with no managed version and break the build, and the resulting
    error names a missing version rather than a wrong artifact id, so it reads
    as a parent misconfiguration. That misdirection is why the rename is
    recorded here instead of being left to be rediscovered.
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    Assumptions: the layering and money prohibitions are enforced as executable
    tests rather than as conventions, so a violation fails the build instead of
    depending on a reviewer noticing it. The rules inherited from
    services/common-lib/src/test/java/com/carddemo/common/architecture/LayeringRulesTest.java
    are that the money path admits no float, no double and no JSON number; that
    no service may import another service's domain package; and that a domain
    package may not import AWS SDK or web types. The third rule is load bearing
    in this module specifically, because it holds both an AWS SDK client and web
    controllers, so its view projections are exactly where such an import would
    otherwise creep in. That test file's path is itself part of the contract and
    must not be relocated, because the rules are discovered by package.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
    =============================================================================
    DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Every entry below is a decision rather than an
    omission, recorded here so that a future reader does not helpfully add one
    back. The dependency set above is complete: nothing further belongs in it.
    =============================================================================

    org.flywaydb:flyway-core and org.flywaydb:flyway-database-postgresql
    Alternatives Considered: (a) own a schema and migrate it, as the other seven
    services do, and as the parent's managed versions for both artifacts would
    make trivial. Rejected because this module is assigned NO owned tables. It
    reads read-only cross-schema views under a role holding SELECT-only grants,
    and both the views and the grants are bootstrapped by
    data-migration/sql/V0__schemas_and_roles.sql. A migration tool here would
    claim ownership of objects this module must not own, and two owners of one
    object is how a schema drifts. (b) add an Aurora read replica so that
    reporting reads never touch the writer. Rejected because a replica adds cost
    and replica-lag semantics for no parity benefit: the baseline read the very
    same VSAM clusters the online programs read, so a replica would introduce a
    staleness window the baseline never had and the golden masters would have no
    way to express. Consequence to act on rather than work around: if a required
    view is missing at runtime that is a data-migration defect to be reported,
    not something to create from here. A src/main/resources/db/migration
    directory in this module is an affirmative defect for the same reason.

    org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-batch
    Refactoring Rationale: the parent manages this at 4.1.0 and batch-service
    declares it, but what changed for reporting is the direction of control.
    This module STARTS a Step Functions state machine execution and returns; it
    does not run the job inline. batch-service owns the Spring Batch
    JobRepository and the batch.batch_run step ledger, which together supply the
    restart semantics that the JCL RESTART= parameter never actually provided in
    the baseline. A second job repository here would be a second, competing
    restart mechanism over the same runs, and two ledgers disagreeing about
    whether a step completed is a worse position than having only one.

    io.awspring.cloud:spring-cloud-aws-starter-sqs
    Alternatives Considered: model the report request as a queued message, the
    way the authorization context models its request and reply exchange.
    Rejected because the baseline submission at app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD L499 to
    L505 was not a request and reply exchange at all: it had no reply queue and
    no correlation identifier, only a one way write. An execution ARN is the
    faithful analogue of a submitted job, whereas adding a queue would invent a
    messaging contract the baseline never had and then oblige this module to
    honour it.

    org.projectlombok:lombok
    Alternatives Considered: generate accessors to keep the DTOs and view
    projections short. Rejected because generated accessors cannot carry the
    Javadoc that the project's explainability rule requires, and the inherited
    Checkstyle gate does not relax skipAnnotations, so generated members would
    be reported as undocumented while offering no source location at which to
    document them. Java 21 record types with explicit constructors give the same
    brevity and leave every member documentable.

    org.mapstruct:mapstruct and its annotation processor
    Alternatives Considered: generate the copybook to DTO mappers. Rejected for
    two independent reasons, either of which alone is sufficient. First, its
    most recent published release is a beta, which is not an acceptable
    dependency anywhere on the money path. Second, and more fundamentally, the
    mapping in this module is not mechanical: it drops FILLER, for example the
    trailing FILLER PIC X(20) at bytes 331 to 350 of app/cpy/COSTM01.CPY; it
    masks the primary account number to its last four digits; it never
    serialises a card verification value; and it applies the COBOL edit masks
    from app/cpy/CVTRA07Y.cpy, namely PIC -ZZZ,ZZZ,ZZZ.ZZ at L30 and
    PIC +ZZZ,ZZZ,ZZZ.ZZ at L54, L60 and L66. Each of those transformations needs
    an inline justification at the mapping site, and a generated mapper has no
    source line on which to hold one.

    io.github.resilience4j:resilience4j-spring-boot3,
    org.springframework.retry:spring-retry, and any circuit breaker starter
    Alternatives Considered: add a resilience library for retry around the state
    machine call and the view reads. Rejected because Spring Framework 7, which
    arrives inside the Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent, moved retry into the framework
    core and already supplies the Retryable annotation, the ConcurrencyLimit
    annotation and a programmatic RetryTemplate with a RetryPolicy. Two API
    facts are easy to get wrong and are recorded here because downstream code in
    this module will use them: the enabling annotation is EnableResilientMethods
    and NOT EnableRetry, and the attribute is maxRetries and NOT maxAttempts, so
    the total number of attempts is one plus the value rather than the value
    itself. resilience4j is additionally ruled out because its published
    artifact targets Spring Boot 3.x. A circuit breaker is omitted separately
    and deliberately: the only synchronous hops here are in-VPC behind an
    internal load balancer with bounded connect and read timeouts, so a breaker
    would add a failure mode, namely an open circuit rejecting healthy traffic,
    without removing one.

    Redis, ElastiCache, Kafka and Kinesis clients
    Alternatives Considered: cache the report and statement results, or stream
    them to consumers. Both rejected because the baseline has neither a cache
    tier nor a streaming tier, so either would be new observable behaviour
    rather than migrated behaviour, and the parity oracle compares this system
    against the baseline's outputs.

    org.testcontainers:testcontainers-localstack
    Trade-offs: the state machine submission could be proven against an emulated
    AWS endpoint, and that accuracy is declined in favour of a Mockito verified
    unit test asserting the StartExecution request this module builds. The
    behaviour worth proving is which input is sent and that a failure propagates
    instead of being ignored, and neither needs a container; adding one would
    pay a container start on every build to verify a service this module does
    not implement.
    =============================================================================
    -->

  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>

      <!--
      Assumptions: the documentation gate is INHERITED and is deliberately not
      restated in this file. The parent aggregator binds maven-checkstyle-plugin
      to the validate phase with the engine overridden to
      com.puppycrawl.tools:checkstyle 13.8.0, configLocation set to
      config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml, failOnViolation true, violationSeverity
      warning and consoleOutput true, so the gate fires on every local build and
      not only in CI. suppressionsLocation is NOT set here because suppressions
      are wired from inside config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml by a
      SuppressionFilter module whose optional attribute is false; setting the
      property at the plugin as well would create a second, competing source for
      that path, with nothing to keep the two in step.

      Nothing in this file skips, relaxes, redeclares or lowers the gate, and
      nothing may. A module local Checkstyle configuration, a skip flag,
      failOnViolation false, failsOnError false, a raised violationSeverity or
      any tolerance of the plugin's return code would each render the
      explainability rule unenforceable for this module while still reporting a
      green build, and a gate that passes without checking is the single failure
      mode the gate exists to prevent.
      -->

      <!--
      Assumptions: this plugin must be declared here rather than inherited, and
      that was verified rather than assumed. In
      org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-parent 4.1.0 the build element
      contains a pluginManagement block and no bare plugins block at all, so
      spring-boot-maven-plugin is MANAGED there and never activated by
      inheritance; activation is per module, which is the standard Spring Boot
      arrangement. The managed declaration already binds the repackage goal and
      pins the version at 4.1.0 by way of spring-boot-dependencies 4.1.0, so no
      version, no execution and no configuration is restated here. The consumer
      of that repackaging is this module's Dockerfile runtime stage, which copies
      the executable jar. Omitting the plugin would still produce a green build
      and a plain library jar, and the failure would surface only much later as a
      container that cannot start, which is why the reasoning sits at the
      declaration instead of being left implicit.

      Trade-offs: finalName is deliberately not set, so the Dockerfile matches
      target/*.jar rather than a pinned file name. A glob is one degree less
      explicit than a pinned name, and that is accepted for two reasons: the
      repackage goal leaves the pre-packaged artifact beside the executable one
      with a .jar.original suffix, which a *.jar glob does not match, so the
      ambiguity the glob appears to introduce does not exist; and a pinned
      finalName would have to be kept in step with the artifact coordinates in
      two separate files, breaking the container build silently the moment they
      diverged.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

    </plugins>
  </build>

</project>
